1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,180
Here's every script
ever written for this channel.

2
00:00:02,410 --> 00:00:08,200
A hundred and fifty-eight videos, almost
one and a half million bytes of text.

3
00:00:08,845 --> 00:00:12,085
Run them through an ordinary compressor
called gzip.

4
00:00:12,405 --> 00:00:15,065
They come out at just
over half a megabyte.

5
00:00:15,585 --> 00:00:17,265
Almost two thirds gone.

6
00:00:17,626 --> 00:00:19,046
And nothing is lost.

7
00:00:19,726 --> 00:00:23,246
Unpack the file, and every script
comes back, letter for letter.

8
00:00:23,953 --> 00:00:25,773
Now compress it again.

9
00:00:26,799 --> 00:00:29,459
It gets bigger. Ninety-eight bytes bigger.

10
00:00:30,138 --> 00:00:32,678
A third time. Ninety-eight bytes bigger.

11
00:00:33,398 --> 00:00:35,378
A fourth. Ninety-eight again.

12
00:00:35,975 --> 00:00:38,335
The first round throws away two thirds.

13
00:00:39,095 --> 00:00:41,135
The second finds nothing at all.

14
00:00:41,275 --> 00:00:43,655
So why can't we just keep compressing?

15
00:00:46,065 --> 00:00:48,365
Let's look inside that second file.

16
00:00:48,985 --> 00:00:50,945
gzip didn't compress anything.

17
00:00:51,545 --> 00:00:55,205
It copied the data across, unchanged,
in sixteen blocks.

18
00:00:55,979 --> 00:00:58,279
Each block got a five-byte label.

19
00:00:58,899 --> 00:01:01,679
Add a small header and a footer,
and you get exactly ninety-eight.

20
00:01:02,938 --> 00:01:05,258
When a compressor finds nothing to remove,

21
00:01:05,498 --> 00:01:10,018
it stores the data as it is,
and adds only what it needs to undo it.

22
00:01:10,543 --> 00:01:14,943
So why did the second round find nothing?
And would a cleverer program?

23
00:01:17,596 --> 00:01:19,656
Text like this is full of repetition.

24
00:01:20,656 --> 00:01:22,676
The same words, the same phrases,

25
00:01:22,856 --> 00:01:25,836
the same little scene markers
at the top of every scene.

26
00:01:26,419 --> 00:01:31,519
In 1977, Jacob Ziv and Abraham Lempel
published a simple trick.

27
00:01:32,279 --> 00:01:33,919
Don't write a repeat again.

28
00:01:34,359 --> 00:01:36,099
Point back to where it was.

29
00:01:36,759 --> 00:01:40,119
Go back three hundred characters,
and copy the next twelve.

30
00:01:40,519 --> 00:01:42,679
A few bytes instead of twelve.

31
00:01:44,743 --> 00:01:49,103
The second trick: give common letters
short codes, and rare ones long codes.

32
00:01:49,809 --> 00:01:54,229
In 1951, David Huffman,
a graduate student at MIT,

33
00:01:54,609 --> 00:01:58,329
could take the final exam,
or write a term paper instead.

34
00:01:59,057 --> 00:02:01,157
The paper, set by Robert Fano,

35
00:02:01,237 --> 00:02:04,817
was to find the most efficient code
of exactly this kind.

36
00:02:05,489 --> 00:02:07,549
For months, Huffman got nowhere.

37
00:02:08,089 --> 00:02:10,549
He gave up,
and started studying for the exam.

38
00:02:11,289 --> 00:02:12,709
Then it came to him.

39
00:02:13,291 --> 00:02:16,231
Take the two rarest symbols,
join them into one,

40
00:02:16,311 --> 00:02:18,811
and repeat until a single tree is left.

41
00:02:19,467 --> 00:02:22,807
He later called it "the most
singular moment of my life."

42
00:02:23,667 --> 00:02:26,967
Fano hadn't told the class
that he'd struggled with it himself,

43
00:02:27,307 --> 00:02:28,927
and so had Claude Shannon.

44
00:02:29,565 --> 00:02:32,065
Huffman's code beat both their methods.

45
00:02:32,665 --> 00:02:35,725
And it can be proven
that no code of its kind does better.

46
00:02:38,055 --> 00:02:42,475
In the early 1990s, Phil Katz put
both tricks together for ZIP files.

47
00:02:42,705 --> 00:02:46,175
gzip uses the same method.

48
00:02:46,751 --> 00:02:51,051
The first round already used up
the patterns gzip knows how to find.

49
00:02:51,689 --> 00:02:53,989
What's left gives it nothing to work with.

50
00:02:54,609 --> 00:02:56,449
To gzip, it looks like noise.

51
00:02:58,749 --> 00:03:01,869
But a cleverer program
might see patterns gzip missed.

52
00:03:02,529 --> 00:03:06,009
Could some program, someday,
shrink anything you give it?

53
00:03:07,149 --> 00:03:07,649
No.

54
00:03:07,729 --> 00:03:10,549
And you don't need to know how it works
to be sure of that.

55
00:03:11,127 --> 00:03:14,207
Take every file
that's exactly three bits long.

56
00:03:14,787 --> 00:03:16,187
There are eight of them.

57
00:03:16,772 --> 00:03:18,672
Now count everything shorter.

58
00:03:19,452 --> 00:03:22,932
Four files of two bits,
two of one bit, and one empty file.

59
00:03:23,569 --> 00:03:24,769
Seven.

60
00:03:25,027 --> 00:03:28,167
Eight files, and seven shorter places
to put them.

61
00:03:28,428 --> 00:03:31,668
If every one got shorter,
two would have to share a place.

62
00:03:32,308 --> 00:03:34,768
And when you unpack it,
which one did you mean?

63
00:03:35,595 --> 00:03:39,455
It's called the pigeonhole principle,
and it holds at any length.

64
00:03:39,735 --> 00:03:42,855
There's always one fewer shorter file
to go around.

65
00:03:43,451 --> 00:03:45,331
So any compressor that takes every file

66
00:03:45,561 --> 00:03:48,031
and shrinks some of them
has to make others bigger.

67
00:03:48,791 --> 00:03:50,751
Not because it's badly written.

68
00:03:51,371 --> 00:03:53,491
There's nowhere else for them to go.

69
00:03:54,332 --> 00:03:57,712
Those ninety-eight bytes
are how gzip pays that price.

70
00:03:58,417 --> 00:04:00,157
Pick any compressor you like.

71
00:04:00,777 --> 00:04:03,877
Of all files of a given size,
it can shrink fewer than one

72
00:04:04,107 --> 00:04:06,557
in two hundred and fifty by a single byte.

73
00:04:07,441 --> 00:04:10,621
Compression works anyway,
because the files we make aren't random.

74
00:04:10,851 --> 00:04:14,941
They're text, pictures and programs,
full of patterns.

75
00:04:16,331 --> 00:04:18,951
That hasn't stopped people
from promising more.

76
00:04:19,284 --> 00:04:23,784
In 1992, WEB Technologies
announced software that could squeeze

77
00:04:24,014 --> 00:04:26,284
almost any data under a kilobyte.

78
00:04:26,364 --> 00:04:29,684
Even its own output, again and again.

79
00:04:30,262 --> 00:04:31,682
Its vice president said the method

80
00:04:31,912 --> 00:04:34,802
was "not subject to the laws
of information theory."

81
00:04:35,482 --> 00:04:37,162
The product never shipped.

82
00:04:39,652 --> 00:04:42,712
So how do photos and music
get so much smaller?

83
00:04:43,332 --> 00:04:45,352
They don't play by this rule at all.

84
00:04:45,737 --> 00:04:48,197
A JPEG doesn't come back identical.

85
00:04:48,857 --> 00:04:50,557
It comes back close enough.

86
00:04:51,197 --> 00:04:54,417
Detail your eye won't miss
is thrown away for good.

87
00:04:55,053 --> 00:04:57,373
Zoom in, and you can see where it went.

88
00:04:58,093 --> 00:05:00,753
That's lossy compression,
and it's a different game.

89
00:05:01,533 --> 00:05:05,653
This video is about the other kind.
Every bit back, exactly.

90
00:05:08,403 --> 00:05:13,603
So for files that do have patterns,
how small can they go? Is there a bottom?

91
00:05:14,567 --> 00:05:19,667
There is. It was found in 1948,
in the same paper behind our video

92
00:05:19,897 --> 00:05:21,527
on error-correcting codes.

93
00:05:22,239 --> 00:05:26,279
There, Claude Shannon showed
how adding redundancy protects a message.

94
00:05:26,859 --> 00:05:30,099
Here, he worked out
how much of it you can take away.

95
00:05:30,763 --> 00:05:36,023
His answer was a number he called entropy:
how hard it is to guess what comes next.

96
00:05:36,769 --> 00:05:39,709
No lossless code can beat that number
on average.

97
00:05:39,789 --> 00:05:42,729
But you can get as close to it
as you like.

98
00:05:45,009 --> 00:05:47,169
So how unpredictable is English?

99
00:05:47,909 --> 00:05:50,869
In 1951, Shannon measured it with a game.

100
00:05:51,528 --> 00:05:53,948
He gave someone a passage
they'd never seen,

101
00:05:54,028 --> 00:05:56,748
and asked them to guess it,
letter by letter.

102
00:05:56,977 --> 00:06:00,657
If a guess was wrong, they guessed again,
until they got it right.

103
00:06:01,319 --> 00:06:03,859
People turned out to be very good at it.

104
00:06:04,559 --> 00:06:09,019
With a hundred letters of context, English
came out at about one bit per letter.

105
00:06:09,651 --> 00:06:12,411
A computer stores each letter
in eight bits.

106
00:06:13,151 --> 00:06:15,231
A human reader needs about one.

107
00:06:15,444 --> 00:06:18,124
That gap is the room compression works in.

108
00:06:18,784 --> 00:06:22,324
gzip got the channel's scripts down
to under three bits a character.

109
00:06:24,734 --> 00:06:27,394
So how close to one bit can a machine get?

110
00:06:28,273 --> 00:06:30,373
There's a prize for exactly that.

111
00:06:30,673 --> 00:06:33,093
It's called the Hutter Prize,
and since 2020,

112
00:06:33,323 --> 00:06:37,353
the task is one file:
a gigabyte of English Wikipedia.

113
00:06:38,205 --> 00:06:40,225
gzip shrinks it to a third.

114
00:06:40,825 --> 00:06:43,665
The best everyday compressors,
to about a fifth.

115
00:06:44,362 --> 00:06:49,182
The record, set in July 2026,
is just over a hundred million bytes.

116
00:06:50,142 --> 00:06:51,342
A tenth.

117
00:06:51,449 --> 00:06:53,489
And the decompressor counts too.

118
00:06:54,229 --> 00:06:58,769
Its size goes into the total, so you can't
hide Wikipedia inside the program.

119
00:06:59,662 --> 00:07:02,802
The fund is five hundred thousand euros.

120
00:07:02,882 --> 00:07:05,782
Every one percent of improvement
pays five thousand.

121
00:07:06,843 --> 00:07:11,123
Marcus Hutter, who funds it, explains why:
"being able to compress well

122
00:07:11,353 --> 00:07:14,183
is closely related
to acting intelligently."

123
00:07:14,447 --> 00:07:17,347
Because the programs at the top
don't hunt for repeats.

124
00:07:18,087 --> 00:07:19,287
They predict.

125
00:07:19,793 --> 00:07:24,413
Before each character, they estimate
how likely every possible next one is.

126
00:07:25,213 --> 00:07:27,533
A good guess costs a fraction of a bit.

127
00:07:28,189 --> 00:07:30,809
It's Shannon's guessing game,
played by a machine.

128
00:07:31,589 --> 00:07:35,829
The best players now use neural networks,
trained to predict what comes next.

129
00:07:36,565 --> 00:07:38,665
It works even on finished files.

130
00:07:39,605 --> 00:07:44,805
In 2016, Dropbox began re-packing
its users' JPEGs with a better predictor.

131
00:07:45,565 --> 00:07:47,525
On average, over a fifth smaller.

132
00:07:48,185 --> 00:07:50,925
Every file came back bit for bit,
and by 2017,

133
00:07:51,155 --> 00:07:54,065
it had saved over forty-six petabytes.

134
00:07:54,605 --> 00:07:57,425
That's over forty-six thousand terabytes.

135
00:07:59,735 --> 00:08:01,355
Then there are the giants.

136
00:08:02,135 --> 00:08:07,335
In 2023, researchers at DeepMind used
a large language model as a compressor.

137
00:08:08,153 --> 00:08:11,433
On small greyscale patches of photos,
it beat PNG:

138
00:08:11,663 --> 00:08:15,453
forty-eight percent of the original size
against fifty-eight.

139
00:08:16,782 --> 00:08:21,722
On recordings of read speech, it beat
FLAC: twenty-one percent against thirty.

140
00:08:22,446 --> 00:08:24,406
But that's without counting the model.

141
00:08:25,166 --> 00:08:27,146
Add its size, and the result comes out

142
00:08:27,376 --> 00:08:29,846
a hundred and forty times
bigger than the data.

143
00:08:30,987 --> 00:08:34,347
A compressor bigger than its data
hasn't saved anything.

144
00:08:34,527 --> 00:08:37,047
The program is part of the description.

145
00:08:39,927 --> 00:08:42,227
And that brings us to the strangest part.

146
00:08:42,907 --> 00:08:45,147
Here are the first million digits of pi.

147
00:08:46,083 --> 00:08:48,103
Feed them to four compressors.

148
00:08:48,403 --> 00:08:52,903
The best gets them down to about four
hundred and twenty-six thousand bytes.

149
00:08:53,427 --> 00:08:56,727
Now give the same compressors
a million random digits.

150
00:08:57,127 --> 00:09:00,167
The same result,
within a tenth of a percent.

151
00:09:01,021 --> 00:09:02,781
But pi isn't made at random.

152
00:09:03,461 --> 00:09:05,421
It follows a mathematical rule.

153
00:09:06,141 --> 00:09:09,461
Here's a program that prints
every one of those million digits.

154
00:09:10,323 --> 00:09:11,893
Three hundred and sixty-two bytes.

155
00:09:12,123 --> 00:09:15,193
Over two thousand times
shorter than the file.

156
00:09:16,094 --> 00:09:19,474
The compressors couldn't see
the mathematical rule behind pi.

157
00:09:19,954 --> 00:09:21,754
They could only see the digits.

158
00:09:24,414 --> 00:09:27,734
One way to measure the information
in data is to ask:

159
00:09:27,894 --> 00:09:30,734
how short can a program be,
and still print it?

160
00:09:31,015 --> 00:09:33,675
That length is called
Kolmogorov complexity,

161
00:09:34,055 --> 00:09:36,275
after one of the men who described it.

162
00:09:36,786 --> 00:09:39,186
Most data has no short program.

163
00:09:39,866 --> 00:09:44,646
It's the pigeonhole principle again: there
aren't enough short programs to go around.

164
00:09:45,375 --> 00:09:48,735
A million random digits
very likely has nothing shorter.

165
00:09:49,175 --> 00:09:50,375
Pi does.

166
00:09:51,041 --> 00:09:54,241
So why not just find
the shortest program for any file?

167
00:09:55,041 --> 00:09:58,861
Because it's been proven that no algorithm
can do that for every file.

168
00:09:59,501 --> 00:10:01,721
Every program gives you an upper limit.

169
00:10:01,801 --> 00:10:05,521
But nothing can always tell you
whether a shorter one exists.

170
00:10:06,201 --> 00:10:08,301
You never know you've reached the bottom.

171
00:10:11,321 --> 00:10:14,401
gzip found the repeats in the scripts,
and nothing more.

172
00:10:15,021 --> 00:10:17,441
It sees bytes, not what the words mean.

173
00:10:18,054 --> 00:10:21,274
To go further, a program
has to predict them better.

174
00:10:22,074 --> 00:10:25,914
And predicting text well starts
to look a lot like understanding it.

175
00:10:26,429 --> 00:10:28,249
So here's a question for you.

176
00:10:28,829 --> 00:10:31,689
If a program could squeeze Wikipedia
smaller than anything else,

177
00:10:31,919 --> 00:10:33,829
would you say it understands it?

178
00:10:34,349 --> 00:10:36,039
Leave your answer in the comments.
